            <ul>
                <li>Early departures help you make the most of your day in Seattle, whether for business or leisure.</li>
                <li>Late departures give flexibility for morning or afternoon plans in Portland, including exploring the city's famous food scene or Powell's Books.</li>
                <li>With multiple daily options, booking is still recommended for peak travel times, weekends, and holidays — though last-minute travel is more feasible than on lower-frequency routes.</li>
            </ul>
            <h2>Understanding the Route Geography</h2>
            <p>The Portland-to-Seattle train route runs through the heart of the Pacific Northwest, traveling north along Interstate 5, with significant scenic diversions. Passengers get spectacular views of the Columbia River Gorge, Mount St. Helens, Mount Rainier, Puget Sound, and the Cascade Range, making the journey more than just transit — it's a relaxed and picturesque experience.</p>
            <p>The route passes through key cities and towns such as Vancouver (WA), Kelso-Longview, Centralia, Olympia-Lacey, and Tacoma, before reaching downtown Seattle. Along the way, travelers can enjoy natural scenery, including dense forests, volcanic peaks, coastal wetlands, and glimpses of urban Pacific Northwest development.</p>
